---
import DemoStatus from '../components/DemoStatus.astro'
import Next from '../components/Next.astro'
import Replay from '../components/Replay'
import Snippet from '../components/Snippet.astro'
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
import { capture } from '../lib/captures'
---

<Page
  page="develop"
  title="Develop"
  description="Your spec becomes types and runnable requests. Write a request or a flow in TypeScript and try it as you write it."
>
  <section class="hero" style="padding-bottom: 24px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">02 · Develop</span>
      <h1 class="display">Your spec, as code you can run.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Specbook generates types and operations from your OpenAPI file. You write requests and flows against
        them in TypeScript, with the editor checking every path, query and body, and run them as you write.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" style="padding-top: 8px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Add your spec.</strong> A file or an address; an address is downloaded and its source recorded.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Write a request.</strong> One call to one operation, and what makes it a pass.</li>
        <li><strong>Chain them into a flow.</strong> What one step returns, the next one uses.</li>
        <li><strong>Run</strong> them in <code>specbook dev</code>, in the terminal, or right here.</li>
      </ol>
      <Snippet file="snippets/install.sh" region="install" title="terminal" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>That is the whole setup. This is what the three commands print, recorded from the real tool:</p>
      </div>
      <Replay client:visible capture={capture('develop-init')} />
      <Replay client:visible capture={capture('develop-add')} />
      <Replay client:visible capture={capture('develop-generate')} />

      <h2 class="title">A request</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          One call to one operation. With no checks of its own it passes on a 2xx response; here it says what
          "found" means.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Snippet file="demo/requests/petstore/get-pet.req.ts" region="request" run="try:petstore/GetPetById" />

      <h2 class="title">A flow</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A flow strings requests together and is <strong>strongly typed</strong>: what a step captures has the
          type the spec gives that response, so a misspelt field is a compile error, not a surprise at run time.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Snippet file="demo/sequences/adopt-pet.seq.ts" region="flow" run="flow:adopt-pet" />
      <p><DemoStatus /></p>

      <h2 class="title">All of it, on a real API</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The same build, for the four APIs of the example project: Stripe, GitHub, the Petstore and a GraphQL
          one, 1,800 operations in all. It is what <code>specbook build</code> publishes.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p><a class="btn primary" href="/demo/">Open the full build ↗</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap"><Next page="develop" /></div>
</Page>
